# Exercise 6: Conditional Independence

## 6.1. Checking Conditional Independence

`rubric={reasoning:2}`

A company uses two automated systems to screen transactions:

- $X=1$ if a **rule-based system** flags a transaction, and $X=0$ otherwise;
- $Y=1$ if a **machine-learning system** flags a transaction, and $Y=0$ otherwise;
- $Z$ indicates the transaction type, where $Z=1$ means **online** and $Z=0$ means **in person**.

Two random variables $X$ and $Y$ are **conditionally independent given** $Z$ if, within each value of $Z$,

$$P(X=x\cap Y=y\mid Z=z)
=
P(X=x\mid Z=z) \times P(Y=y\mid Z=z).$$

Suppose the following probabilities are known:

| Transaction type | $P(X=1\mid Z=z)$ | $P(Y=1\mid Z=z)$ | $P(X=1\cap Y=1\mid Z=z)$ |
|:--:|:--:|:--:|:--:|
| Online ($Z=1$) | $0.40$ | $0.50$ | $0.20$ |
| In person ($Z=0$) | $0.20$ | $0.30$ | $0.06$ |

Determine whether $X$ and $Y$ are **conditionally independent given** $Z$.

For each transaction type, compare

$$P(X=1\cap Y=1\mid Z=z)$$

with

$$P(X=1\mid Z=z) \times P(Y=1\mid Z=z).$$

Then, state your conclusion in **no more than 2 sentences**.

> **Heads-up:** Since $X$ and $Y$ are binary, their conditional joint distribution has only four possible pairs:
>
> $$(0,0),\ (0,1),\ (1,0),\ (1,1).$$
>
> Once the conditional marginal probabilities of $X$ and $Y$ are known, checking the factorization for $(X,Y)=(1,1)$ is enough in this setting. If
>
> $$P(X=1\cap Y=1\mid Z=z)
> =
> P(X=1\mid Z=z) \times P(Y=1\mid Z=z),$$
>
> then the probabilities of the other three pairs are determined by subtraction and also satisfy the corresponding factorization.

